{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underprediction: when the last 5–10 days show a persistent rise (higher highs/higher lows), add a positive level shift to the whole path. In the examples, the model is systematically ~+5 to +15 EUR too low from ~day 7 onward.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: apply a ramping correction that increases with forecast horizon (small adjustment days 1–3, larger days 7–30). The error grows materially with lead time, indicating the model fails to carry trend/acceleration forward.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the model reacts too slowly to regime changes (breakout from ~65–70 to mid/high 70s and 80s). Use a momentum trigger: if (last price > 20-day MA) and (10-day slope positive), increase projected slope (not just level).",
    "Premature mean reversion: forecasts repeatedly bend back toward ~68–70 even while realized prices keep trending up. Suppress mean-reversion terms when realized volatility is rising and price is making new 20–30 day highs.",
    "Post-spike handling is asymmetric: the model often assumes pullbacks after strength, but realized series continues to grind higher. After an upside breakout, cap downward day-to-day corrections for the next ~5–10 days (i.e., enforce non-negative drift unless clear reversal signal appears)."
  ]
}
